   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of Member States to formulate and implement national employment policies in response to the COVID-19 crisis</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. In 2022, the Government of the Republic of Namibia has established and Inter-Ministerial Technical Working Group to spearhead the formulation and implementation of the 3rd National Employment Policy (NEP): 
a. Over the period 2022-2023 and as part of the implementation of the review and evaluation of NEP 2 capacities to formulate a new generation National Employment Policy (NEP) integrating cross-government approach, with well-functioning coordination mechanisms among ministries, social partners, and other stakeholders has been established.
b. To inform better the formulation of the NEP 3 the Inter-Ministerial Technical Working Group together with social partners, and other stakeholders including the academia had its capacity strengthened to conduct sectoral and national Employment Impact Assessments (EmpIAs) to promote the creation of more and better jobs.

2. Over the period 2022-2023 the Government of the Republic of Namibia developed and adopted an Employment Public Expenditure Review and Pro-Employment and Gender Budgeting Toolkits:
a. The Employment Public Expenditure Review assessed the extent to which the national budget allocations and expenditure supported employment targeting and job creation.
b. The Pro-Employment and Gender Budgeting Toolkits will enable the Ministry of Labour Industrial Relations and Employment Creation and other government departments to advocate for increased budgetary allocations towards job creation and the implementation of EmpIAs to assess government programmes and projects. 1. Through collaboration between Regular Budget Supplementary Account (RBSA), Regular Budget Technical Cooperation (RBTC) and ILO/EU STRENGTHEN 2 projects, conducted capacity building workshops for the Inter-Ministerial Technical Working Group on NEPs in February 2022 and EmpIAs in April 2023.
a. In 2023, one from the Ministry of Labour Industrial Relations and Employment Creation participated in the ILO/ITC Turin Course on Jobs Measurement and Employment Impact Assessment course with ILO support.
b. In 2023, five (5) officials participated in the ILO/ITC Turin Youth Employment Academy to gain knowledge by learning from experiences of other countries to address the youth employment challenge.
2. The ILO provided financial and technical support through Regular Budget Supplementary Account (RBSA), Regular Budget Technical Cooperation (RBTC) and the UN SDG Fund Joint Programme resources for the consultants that drafted the Expenditure Review and the Toolkits.
a. The ILO also supported the consultations and validation workshops in April 2023.</narrative>
   </description>
